lesson k-more-fewer-the-same v=1 format=teach grade=0 unit=3 subject=maths levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "More, fewer and the same"
  goal "Compare two groups by matching them one to one, say which has more or fewer, and make them the same."

  look {
    scene 12x9 {
      props top count=4 prop=apple at=canvas(1, 1)
      props bottom count=3 prop=star below=top gap=1
    }
    say """
      Put one star under each apple. One apple has no star, so there are more apples and fewer
      stars. If every apple had a star and nothing was left over, there would be the same number.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice k.same-or-not count=2 seed=2
    level easy {
      worked k.fewer a=5 b=2 level=medium
    }
    practice k.fewer count=2 seed=3
    practice k.tallest-tower count=2 seed=4
    practice k.how-many-more count=2 seed=5 easy-count=1
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show k.longer-row k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show k.share-the-same a=5 b=1 easy-a=4 easy-b=2 hard-a=6 hard-b=2
  }
  remember {
    say "Match them one to one. The group with some left over has more."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Read each question aloud. Matching one to one comes before counting here: put a finger on an
    apple and a finger on a star, and move along together. The word fewer is new to many children,
    so say "not as many" beside it. The two-star question is the classic trap of a spread-out row,
    which looks like more; if the child agrees with Ben, count both rows together. Off the page, lay
    out a cup for each person at the table and ask whether there are enough. England, Reception, ELG
    Numerical Patterns (recalled, not fetched): "Compare quantities up to 10 in different contexts,
    recognising when one quantity is greater than, less than or the same as the other quantity".
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the groups to compare differ by two or more, one is worked in the teacher's pen first, and Ada needs one more apple."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the groups differ by one or are the same, the towers are close in height, Ada needs three more, and the three-star moves two apples."
  }
}
